================================================================================
IMPORTANT: DON'T LOG DURING JSX RENDER
================================================================================

The supplied lesson used:

{console.log(
  `Checkbox rendered...`
)}


inside JSX.


Avoid this pattern.


Why?

Rendering should primarily calculate UI.

A render may occur more than once during development.

Console output from render is therefore noisy and can be misleading if you
treat every log as proof of one user interaction.


Prefer temporary logs in:

• Event handlers

• Explicit debugging code


rather than:

JSX rendering expressions.


================================================================================
IMPORTANT: STATE UPDATES ARE REQUESTS
================================================================================

Consider:

setGroceryItems(
  nextItems
)

console.log(
  groceryItems
)


Do NOT assume the second statement immediately contains the new React state.

The setter requests a state update.

React applies the update and renders again.


If you need to inspect what you are about to return inside a functional setter:

setGroceryItems(
  previousItems => {

    const nextItems =
      previousItems.map(...)


    console.log(
      nextItems
    )


    return nextItems
  }
)


For normal production code, remove such debugging afterward.


